# 1. H2 DB Backup — Single File Retention Strategy

## Background

The application uses an H2 in-memory database and performs a scheduled backup every 15 minutes to prevent data loss on application crash or restart. Each scheduled run creates a new backup file with a timestamp or incremental name, causing the backup folder to accumulate an unbounded number of files over time.

## Problem Statement

The backup folder grows continuously. After a few hours, dozens of backup files exist, consuming disk space unnecessarily. Only the most recent complete backup is ever needed for restore.

## Functional Requirements

| # | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-1.1 | The backup scheduler must write the new backup to a **temporary file** first. |
| FR-1.2 | Only after the new backup file is **fully and successfully written** must the previous backup file be deleted. |
| FR-1.3 | At any point in time, the backup folder must contain **at most 2 backup files** — the current backup and briefly the temp file during the swap window. |
| FR-1.4 | If the new backup write **fails**, the previous backup must **NOT be deleted**. The old backup must be preserved as the last known good backup. |
| FR-1.5 | The backup file must be named consistently — a **fixed filename** (e.g., `release-tracker-backup.mv.db`) — not a timestamped name. |
| FR-1.6 | Log the backup lifecycle at INFO level: backup start, backup complete, old file deleted. |
| FR-1.7 | Log any failure at ERROR level with the exception detail. |
| FR-1.8 | The backup interval (15 minutes) must remain configurable via application properties. |

## Backup Swap Algorithm

```
Step 1:  Write new backup to → backup-temp.mv.db
Step 2:  Verify temp file exists AND size > 0
Step 3:  If verification passes:
             Delete → release-tracker-backup.mv.db  (previous)
             Rename → backup-temp.mv.db  to  release-tracker-backup.mv.db
Step 4:  If verification fails:
             Delete backup-temp.mv.db (cleanup)
             Log ERROR
             Retain previous release-tracker-backup.mv.db unchanged
```

## Acceptance Criteria

```
AC-1.1
GIVEN the backup scheduler fires
WHEN the backup starts
THEN the new backup is written to a temporary file first
AND the existing backup file is NOT touched during write

AC-1.2
GIVEN the new backup completes successfully
WHEN the temp file is verified (exists, size > 0)
THEN the old backup file is deleted
AND the temp file is renamed to the fixed backup filename
AND the backup folder contains exactly 1 backup file

AC-1.3
GIVEN the new backup write fails midway
WHEN the scheduler handles the error
THEN the temp file is cleaned up
AND the previous backup file is retained unchanged
AND an ERROR is logged with the exception detail

AC-1.4
GIVEN multiple backup cycles have run (e.g., 10 cycles over 2.5 hours)
WHEN the backup folder is inspected
THEN it contains at most 2 files (current backup + temp during active swap window)
AND no timestamped accumulation of files exists

AC-1.5
GIVEN the application crashes after a successful backup
WHEN the application restarts
THEN it restores from the single backup file successfully

AC-1.6
GIVEN each backup cycle
WHEN the lifecycle completes
THEN INFO logs exist for: backup start, backup success, old file deleted

AC-1.7
GIVEN the backup interval property is set to a different value
WHEN the application starts
THEN the backup runs at the configured interval
```

## Edge Cases

- Backup folder does not exist on first run → create it automatically, log INFO
- No previous backup exists (first ever backup) → skip delete step, write directly
- Disk full during backup write → log ERROR, do not delete old backup
- Rename operation fails after delete → log CRITICAL, operator intervention required

